{
  "commit_id": "3d50677e18e9b7e4b655f11717efcb957170d455",
  "event": "COMMENT",
  "body": "Reviewed via a wakil-specific PR-reviewer persona (staff-eng lens + live ADR/dev-doc cross-checks). Verified the PR's own claims independently: checked out the branch and ran `ruff check .` (clean) and `pytest -q -m \"not eval\"` (451 passed), and exercised the migration chain through the repo's legacy-DB-stamp/upgrade tests rather than just reading the migration file.\n\n**Overall:** clean, well-tested diff, no blockers. This correctly finishes what ADR 0006 explicitly deferred as follow-up (confirmed by reading the ADR's \"Consequences\" section), stays compliant with ADR 0007 (no backlink prose written to Markdown), doesn't implicate ADR 0008 (traversal is a fixed deterministic SQL query, no runtime-decided dispatch), and correctly follows the free-string-over-enum precedent from ADR 0013/0014 for `predicate`. The explicit `Relationship` cleanup in the workspace prune path is real, necessary defensive code (there's no `PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON` anywhere in `database.py`, so it's not dead boilerplate), and the recursive CTE's SQL interpolation only selects between fixed, pre-validated fragments — no injection surface.\n\nA few line comments below — all minor/cosmetic or forward-looking, nothing blocking.",
  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "src/wakil/cli/main.py",
      "line": 222,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Minor: `workspace` isn't checked for `None` before `workspace.id` is used below — if the lookup misses, this raises an unhandled `AttributeError` instead of a clean CLI error (the project's UX bar elsewhere asks that errors tell the user what to do next). Not a regression this PR introduces — the identical unguarded pattern already exists in `query_service.py:44`, `git_service.py:378`, and `schema_migrate_service.py` for the same query, and it's practically unreachable since `_resolve_workspace`/`init_workspace` guarantee the row exists. Not worth blocking on, but if any of these ever get hardened it'd be good to do all of them together."
    },
    {
      "path": "src/wakil/app/graph_service.py",
      "line": 161,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "The `ORDER BY w.via_predicate, w.direction LIMIT 1` tie-break (here and at line 164) deterministically picks `'in'` over `'out'` (alphabetical) when a note is reachable both ways at the same depth. Confirmed intentional and correct — `test_both_direction_merges_and_dedupes` locks in \"one row, not two\" — but the rendered table (`print_traversal_result`) gives no visual indication that a note might be linked both ways. A user running `--direction both` won't see that a relationship is mutual. Cosmetic, not a bug."
    },
    {
      "path": "src/wakil/app/graph_service.py",
      "line": 150,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "The `walk` CTE dedupes exact `(note_id, depth, predicate, direction)` tuples, not per-node reachability, so a densely cross-linked cluster can produce multiple path-rows per note before `best_depth`'s `MIN()` collapses them. `MAX_TRAVERSAL_DEPTH = 5` (line 25) is the acknowledged mitigation, and this tradeoff is already flagged in the originating proposal doc as deliberately deferred — not something to gate merge on, but worth a profiling pass once this runs against the real ~2,700-note vault with hub notes."
    },
    {
      "path": "tests/integration/test_relationships_cli.py",
      "line": 78,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Optional: `--depth 0` is unit-tested at `graph_service.traverse()` (`test_bad_depth_raises`) but doesn't have a CLI-level counterpart the way this bad-`--direction` test does. Low risk since both share the same `TraversalError` → `typer.Exit(1)` handling, but a symmetric CLI test here would close the gap cheaply."
    }
  ]
}

